# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-plugin-epure-windows-validation — run --epure against real AutoCAD and BricsCAD on the Windows runner
#+STATUS: BLOCKED
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: cad-runtime
#+DEPENDS: windows-access, alfe-plugin-epure
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-plugin-epure-windows

* Context

=alfe-plugin-epure= is asserted only against the mock CAD, =--print-command=
and an OS override: no Windows, no EPURE install, no CAD in the session that
wrote it. Two assumptions came from the legacy wrapper and were never
re-verified:

1. a =._SCRIPT= nested in a =/b= script gives control back to the outer
   script, so the line after it — =(load run-common.lsp)= — runs;
2. under AutoCAD COM, =SendCommand("._SCRIPT …")= followed by
   =SendCommand("(load …)")= runs them in that order.

Also unverified: dropping =/Automation= for EPURE is right (EPURE loads a
CUIX and menus); the default control-script paths for the current EPURE
year; the GUI staying usable after the run-time is loaded.

* Proposal

On the Windows runner with EPURE installed: run =alfe --bricscad --epure
-x '(princ "ok")'= and the AutoCAD equivalent; keep the workdir
(=--keep-workdir=) and read =run.scr= / =bridge-*.vbs= / =debug.log=. Fix
the plug-in accordingly (the alternative to a nested =._SCRIPT= is COM
=SendCommand=, already supported by the same hooks). Record the outcome in
the user manual's EPURE section.

* Acceptance criteria

- Both CADs run an =-x= under EPURE and exit with the script's status.
- The manual states what was verified, on which CAD/EPURE versions.
